<h2 class=3D"      new--design ">
   =20
   =20
        Devices Most Vulnerable To Hacking
   =20

</h2>






</div>


   =20
   =20
    <div class=3D"C15-Title">





<h3 class=3D"       ">
   =20
   =20
       =20

   =20
   =20
        Smart devices
   =20

</h3>






</div>


   =20
   =20
    <div class=3D"C32-Text text parbase">
  <p>Smart devices, such as smartphones, are lucrative targets for hackers.=
 Android devices, in particular, have a more open-source and inconsistent s=
oftware development process than Apple devices, which puts them at risk of =
data theft or corruption. However, hackers are increasingly targeting the m=
illions of devices connected to the&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://www.fortinet.co=
m/resources/cyberglossary/iot">Internet of Things (IoT)</a>.</p>


</div>


   =20
   =20
    <div class=3D"C15-Title">





<h3 class=3D"       ">
   =20
   =20
        Webcams
   =20

</h3>






</div>


   =20
   =20
    <div class=3D"C32-Text text parbase">
  <p>Webcams built into computers are a common hacking target, mainly becau=
se hacking them is a simple process. Hackers typically gain access to a com=
puter using a&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://www.fortinet.com/resources/cybergloss=
ary/remote-access-trojan">Remote Access Trojan (RAT)</a>&nbsp;in&nbsp;<a hr=
ef=3D"https://www.fortinet.com/resources/cyberglossary/rootkit">rootkit</a>=
&nbsp;malware, which allows them to not only spy on users but also read the=
ir messages, see their browsing activity, take screenshots, and hijack thei=
r webcam.</p>


</div>


   =20
   =20
    <div class=3D"C15-Title">





<h3 class=3D"       ">
   =20
   =20
        Routers
   =20

</h3>






</div>


   =20
   =20
    <div class=3D"C32-Text text parbase">
  <p>Hacking routers enables an attacker to gain access to data sent and re=
ceived across them and networks that are accessed on them. Hackers can also=
 hijack a router to carry out wider malicious acts such as distributed deni=
al-of-service (DDoS) attacks,&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://www.fortinet.com/reso=
urces/cyberglossary/what-is-dns">Domain Name System (DNS) spoofing</a>, or =
cryptomining.</p>


</div>


   =20
   =20
    <div class=3D"C15-Title">





<h3 class=3D"       ">
   =20
   =20
        Email
   =20

</h3>






</div>


   =20
   =20
    <div class=3D"C32-Text text parbase">
  <p>Email is one of the most common targets of&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://www=
.fortinet.com/blog/threat-research/bluekeep-rdp-attacks-starting-patch-now"=
>cyberattacks</a>. It is used to spread malware and ransomware and as a tac=
tic for phishing attacks, which enable attackers to target victims with mal=
icious attachments or links.&nbsp;</p>


</div>


   =20
   =20
    <div class=3D"C15-Title">





<h3 class=3D"       ">
   =20
   =20
        Jailbroken phones
   =20

</h3>






</div>


   =20
   =20
    <div class=3D"C32-Text text parbase">
  <p>Jailbreaking a phone means removing restrictions imposed on its operat=
ing system to enable the user to install applications or other software not=
 available through its official app store. Aside from being a violation of =
the end-user=E2=80=99s license agreement with the phone developer, jailbrea=
king exposes many vulnerabilities. Hackers can target jailbroken phones, wh=
ich allows them to steal any data on the device but also extend their attac=
k to connected networks and systems.</p>


</div>




	</div>
</section>
</div>



